use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Modality:
    description:
        Modality blocks the rest of the page while a panel, such as a dialog,
        is open, so the user deals with the panel before anything else. It
        belongs to the component that opens the panel.

    focusContainment:
        Focus moves into the panel when it opens and stays there. Tab and
        Shift+Tab move through what's inside, wrapping round from the last
        thing to the first and back. The keyboard can't move focus anywhere
        outside the panel. If nothing inside can take focus, the panel itself
        takes it.

    initialFocus:
        By default, focus goes to the first thing in the panel that can take
        it. The component can pick something else, such as the least harmful
        button in a "Delete this?" panel, or the top of the panel when its
        content is long and the user should start reading there. Opening
        without moving focus at all is only for panels the user didn't ask
        for, and even then the panel is announced.

    inertBackground:
        Everything outside the panel can't be clicked, focused, or typed into,
        and is hidden from assistive tools, so a screen reader user can't
        wander out of the panel by accident. Popups opened from inside the
        panel count as part of it, even if they are drawn elsewhere on the
        page. How the rest of the page is blocked is up to the chosen library.

    scrollLock:
        The page behind the panel doesn't scroll while it is open, whether by
        wheel, touch, or keyboard. The panel's own content can still scroll.
        Locking the page doesn't make its content jump sideways when the
        scrollbar goes away. When the panel closes, the page is where the user
        left it. The component can leave scrolling on, such as for a panel
        that covers only part of the screen.

    backdrop:
        Something usually covers the page behind the panel, such as a dimmed
        layer, to show it can't be used. Whether clicking it closes the panel
        is up to the component, as described in Dismissal.

    focusReturn:
        When the panel closes, focus goes back to whatever opened it, or to
        somewhere the component chooses if that is gone. The rest of the page
        becomes usable again at the same moment.

    nesting:
        A blocking panel can open another on top of it. Only the top one can
        be used; the one under it is blocked like the rest of the page. When
        the top one closes, the one under it can be used again and focus goes
        back into it. The page stays locked until the last one closes.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't decide how the panel closes. That's Dismissal
        - Not for panels that leave the rest of the page usable, such as a
            Popover
